---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import guide from '../../data/sim/guide.json';
import type { GuideIndex, TheoryIndex } from '../../sim/guide/data';

// A one-page pilot protocol: the recording at release, with the predictions fixed by the field guide's run.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const g = guide as unknown as GuideIndex;
const cell = (th: TheoryIndex, key: string) => th.traits.find((x) => x.id === key)?.cell ?? '—';
---
<Doc
  title="Recording a knot as it lets go: a pilot protocol"
  kicker="Pilot protocol · draft"
  current="research"
  description="A one-page pilot protocol: laser speckle, single-motor-unit EMG and ultrasound at the moment a knot lets go, with each theory's prediction fixed in advance."
>
  <div class="pilot">
    <p class="meta mono">Draft 1 · 28 September 2026 · predictions fixed by the field guide run {g.run.inputs} ·{' '}
      <a href={`${base}/research/preprint/`}>the preprint</a> · <a href={`${base}/research/pilot.pdf`}>PDF</a></p>

    <h2>Aim</h2>
    <p>To record, for the first time, what happens in the tissue at the moment a knot lets go, and so tell apart the seven
      mechanisms proposed for knots. Each has been run as a model from its own physiology; at a release each would leave a
      different trace. This pilot tests feasibility and the direction of those traces, not their size.</p>

    <h2>What each theory predicts</h2>
    <table class="pred">
      <thead><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Recorded at release</th><th>Under a resting hand</th><th>Where knots sit</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {g.theories.map((th) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="glyph">{th.glyph}</span> {th.name}</th>
            <td>{cell(th, 'record')}</td>
            <td>{cell(th, 'hand')}</td>
            <td>{cell(th, 'where')}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">From the models' runs in {g.run.settings} settings each; a perforator's sleeve adds that the layers slide again
      only once moved, not at the release itself.</p>

    <h2>Participants and setting</h2>
    <p>Twelve adults who report knots in the upper back and notice their releases. Excluded: cardiovascular or peripheral vascular
      disease, neuropathy, pregnancy, current psychiatric crisis, and any history of severe difficulty with meditation or bodywork.
      Ethics approval and informed consent; a quiet laboratory at 24 °C after 20 minutes' acclimatisation, seated.</p>

    <h2>Instruments</h2>
    <p>Laser speckle contrast imaging of skin flow over a 10 × 10 cm field of the upper back; high-density surface EMG (a
      64-electrode grid) over the upper trapezius, decomposed into single motor units; ultrasound (B-mode, colour Doppler and
      shear-strain imaging of the layers' sliding); a pressure sensor under the practitioner's hand; and a button the participant
      presses at each felt release.</p>

    <h2>Procedure (about 90 minutes)</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Map.</strong> The participant marks 3–5 knots; a blinded examiner maps perforators by colour Doppler; ultrasound
        notes the layer of any bump; two control points without knots are marked.</li>
      <li><strong>Baseline.</strong> Five minutes at rest, all instruments recording.</li>
      <li><strong>Release trials,</strong> each knot in random order, at the participant's usual pace: a practitioner's hand
        resting at comfortable pressure for up to a minute, then lifted; attention without touch for up to three minutes; a roller
        over the knot for up to three minutes. The same hand and roller on a control point (the pressed sham). Three minutes'
        rest between trials.</li>
      <li><strong>After each release:</strong> ultrasound of the layers' sliding at 0, 5 and 10 minutes, and after the
        participant moves the shoulder.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Outcomes</h2>
    <p>Locked to each button press (30 s either side): skin flow at the knot against the pressed sham; any motor unit falling
      silent under the grid; the layers' shear strain at the knot; whether knots sit on perforators more often than control
      points; and how long after a release the layers slide again. Twelve people with 3–5 knots give 40–60 release events,
      enough to see the direction of each trace.</p>

    <h2>Reading the result</h2>
    <p>Skin flow flooding in at the knot and not at the sham: perforators. Flow rising slowly over tens of seconds: the vascular
      latch. A motor unit falling silent at the felt release: the motor switch. Stiffness falling in the muscle under the press:
      trigger points. The layers sliding again at the release: densification; only after the shoulder moves: a perforator's
      sleeve. Nothing in the tissue: perception, or a nerve, which a local anaesthetic at the point would then test (in a
      clinical setting). Different knots may give different traces: the pilot is designed to find that too.</p>

    <h2>Safety</h2>
    <p>Ordinary breathing throughout: no breath-holds or forceful breathing. Comfortable pressure only, measured, and nothing on the
      front of the neck. Any trial stops at the first sign of distress. Deep somatic and contemplative work can cause lasting
      difficulties; see the site's <a href={`${base}/about/#moderation`}>moderation guidance</a>. The predictions and analysis
      are registered before any recording.</p>
  </div>
</Doc>

<style>
  .pilot {
    max-width: 720px;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.58;
  }
  .meta {
    font-size: 9.5px;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 6px;
  }
  .mono {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 14px 0 3px;
  }
  p,
  ol {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
  }
  ol {
    padding-left: 18px;
  }
  li {
    margin-bottom: 3px;
  }
  .pred {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: 11.5px;
    margin: 4px 0 2px;
  }
  .pred th,
  .pred td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 4px 8px 4px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .pred thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 9px;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .pred tbody th {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 600;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .glyph {
    font-family: var(--font-cjk);
    color: var(--jade);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .note {
    font-size: 11px;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  @media print {
    .pilot {
      font-size: 10.5px;
      line-height: 1.42;
      max-width: none;
    }
    h2 {
      font-size: 11px;
      margin: 8px 0 2px;
    }
    .pred {
      font-size: 9.5px;
    }
  }
</style>
